/-!
# The Wythoff semifan of a generalized Coxeter chamber (#24)

V. Alexeev, P. Engel, A. Thompson, *Stable pair compactification of moduli of K3 surfaces of
degree 2*, arXiv:1903.09742v3, §4D:

* **Definition 4.16** (p. 26). Let `N` be a hyperbolic lattice with positive cone `C`, `W` a
  discrete group generated by reflections in vectors `r_k`, `k ∈ K`, with `r_k² < 0` and
  `r_k · r_k' ≥ 0` for `k ≠ k'`, and `K = ⋂_k H⁺_{r_k} ∩ C` the fundamental domain. Split
  `K = I ⊔ J` into *active* and *inactive* mirrors; a face `K ∩ ⋂_{j ∈ V} r_j^⊥` is
  *irrelevant* if `V ⊆ J`. Let `W_J = ⟨w_j, j ∈ J⟩` and `L = ⋃_{h ∈ W_J} h(K)`. The
  **generalized Coxeter semifan** `F^semi` has maximal cones `g(L)`, `g ∈ W`.
* **Proposition 4.17** (p. 26). (1) `L = ⋂_{i ∈ I, h ∈ W_J} H⁺_{h(r_i)}`, so `L` is convex;
  (2) the stabilizer of `L` in `W` is `W_J`; (3) the support of `F^semi` is `C`; (4) the cones
  of `F^semi` are `g(F)` for `g ∈ W` and the *relevant* faces `F` of `K`.
* **Remark 4.19** (p. 27). When `J` is elliptic (`W_J` finite) the semifan is a fan, and it is
  the normal fan of the Wythoff permutahedron `Conv(W.q)`. In general (p. 27) "the cone `L`
  has infinitely many faces and an infinite stabilizer group".

## What this file constructs

This is the *general* coarsened semifan family of Definition 4.16 and Proposition 4.17(4), over
an arbitrary finite family of linear functionals `φ : ι → M →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ` (the simple-root functionals
`r_k · -`), an active/inactive split `J`, and a pair of subgroups `W_J ≤ W` of `M ≃ₗ[ℝ] M`:

* `aetChamber φ` is `K = {x | ∀ k, 0 ≤ φ_k x}`,
* `aetChamberFace φ V` is the face `F_V = K ∩ ⋂_{i ∈ V} ker φ_i`,
* `AET.IsRelevant J V` is `∃ i ∈ V, i ∉ J` — AET's "relevant face" `V ⊄ J`,
* `aetGeneralizedChamber φ W_J` is `L = ⋃_{h ∈ W_J} h(K)`,
* `aetGeneralizedChamberFace φ W_J V` is the truncation `L_V = L ∩ ⋂_{i ∈ V} ker φ_i`,
* `aetWythoffSemifan φ J W W_J` is the family `F^semi` of Proposition 4.17(4): the
  `W`-translates of `L` together with the `W`-translates of the relevant chamber faces.

Nothing in these definitions is finitary: `W_J` is an arbitrary subgroup of `M ≃ₗ[ℝ] M`, so the
family can be infinite, `L` need not be finitely generated, and no finiteness of `W_J` is
assumed anywhere. **The finite-`W_J` specialization of Remark 4.19 is a different theorem and is
not asserted here**: this file does not claim that the family is a fan, that its cones are
rational polyhedral, or that any cone is finitely generated.

## Relation to the `RootPairing` chamber layer that landed with this issue

`LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.FanCoarsening` (as of #24's accepted increment) and
`LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.AETHypotheses` state Definition 4.16 and Proposition 4.17(1) for a
`RootPairing ι ℝ M N` with `[Finite ι]`. That formalism cannot carry the infinite-parabolic case
that Proposition 4.17 and Remark 4.19 are about: with `[Finite ι]` and `P.IsRootSystem` the Weyl
group embeds in `Equiv.Perm ι`, so every standard parabolic is finite (the scope note recorded in
`AETHypotheses`). The present file therefore states Definition 4.16 for an arbitrary finite family
of linear functionals `φ : ι → M →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ` and an arbitrary pair of subgroups `W_J ≤ W` of
`M ≃ₗ[ℝ] M`, which is the form in which both the `RootPairing` case and the explicit infinite
matrix datum are instances. The two `RootPairing`-to-here identifications are
`aetChamber (φ := B.form (P.root ·)) = fundamentalChamber P b B` and the corresponding identity
for `aetGeneralizedChamber`; the hypothesis `hL` below is the per-chamber form of
`generalizedChamber_subset_activeHalfspaceIntersection`. They are not restated as lemmas here
because that layer is not on the base of this commit; the instantiation that *is* carried out is
`WythoffInfiniteParabolicExample`, where `hL` is discharged for an infinite `W_J`.

## The semifan axioms, stated and proved

A semifan's axiom list is made explicit as the structure `IsWythoffFamily` (no axiom is a field
of a definition of the family itself). For `aetWythoffSemifan`:

* `aetChamberFace_isConeFace_chamber` : `F_V` is a face of `K`;
* `aetChamberFace_isConeFace_aetGeneralizedChamber` : **the relevant-face statement** — for `V`
  consisting of *active* mirrors `F_V` is a face of `L`; this is where AET's Proposition 4.17(1)
  forward inclusion enters, as the hypothesis `hL`;
* `aetChamberFace_singleton_not_isConeFace` : **the exclusion** — a face cut out by a single
  *inactive* mirror is *not* a face of `L`; this is AET's "irrelevant" criterion and is the
  precise sense in which the family would be wrong if irrelevant faces were included;
* `aetWythoffSemifan_faceClosure` : **face closure** — every face of a chamber-face cone of the
  family cut out by simple mirrors is again a cone of the family, and every truncation of a
  maximal cone by active mirrors is a face of that maximal cone, exhibited as the union over
  `W_J` of the faces of the individual chambers;
* `aetGeneralizedChamberFace_inter`, `aetChamberFace_inter`, `isConeFace_inter` :
  **intersection** — the truncations of a maximal cone are closed under intersections, the
  intersection of two is a face of each, and the intersection of two chamber faces is the
  chamber face of the union;
* `aetWythoffSemifan_smul_mem`, `aetWythoffSemifan_equivariant` : **full `W`-equivariance** —
  the family is stable under *all* of `W`, not only under `W_J`;
* `aetWythoffSemifan_orbits_finite` and `aetWythoffSemifan_chamberFace_finite_of_chambers` :
  **local finiteness** — finitely many `W`-orbits of cones (so the family is finite modulo `W`),
  and, on a set meeting finitely many chambers, only finitely many cones of chamber-face type
  meet it.

### Local finiteness, and why it is not point-finiteness

For a `W`-invariant family, point-local finiteness is *false* as soon as `W_J` is infinite: an
interior point `x` of `K` lies in `g(L)` exactly when `g⁻¹x ∈ h(K)` for some `h ∈ W_J`, i.e.
exactly when `g ∈ W_J` (times the chamber stabilizer). The consumer file
`WythoffInfiniteParabolicExample` proves this on an explicit infinite-`W_J` datum
(`mem_infinitelyMany_maximalCones`), so the correct local-finiteness statement is the one proved
here: finite modulo the group, and locally finite on the chamber tiling. A statement of point
finiteness would silently assume that `W_J` is finite, i.e. it would replace the semifan by a fan.

